        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Do you need a mat under a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually. A walking pad is light, but it moves slightly with every step, which can scuff hardwood and laminate, creep across smooth floors and draw carpet fibre into the motor. A thin mat holds it still, protects the floor and catches dust and sweat. On bare concrete, a mat is optional.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What size mat do I need for a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One a couple of inches longer than the pad at each end and, ideally, a few inches wider on each side so a sideways step off lands on the mat. Measure the pad's full footprint, not its belt. Many walking pads are roughly 50 to 60 inches long and 20 to 25 inches wide, but check yours.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How thick should a walking pad mat be?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              About 4 to 6 millimetres, or roughly 0.16 to 0.25 inches. That protects the floor and dampens vibration without raising the deck enough to change your desk height or letting a light pad rock. Thick, soft mats suit heavy full-size treadmills better than walking pads.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a mat stop my walking pad bothering the neighbours downstairs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It helps with the thud of footfalls and frame vibration, but not with motor hum, and it will not silence a heavy walker. Isolation pads under the feet, a softer walking style and walking during the day rather than late at night usually make a bigger difference.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a yoga mat under a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is not ideal. Yoga mats are soft, grippy on top and often thicker than an equipment mat, so a walking pad can rock on one and the feet can tear the surface. A dense equipment mat is better, and costs about the same.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad mat safe on vinyl or LVP floors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check your floor maker's care guide first. Some vinyl floor makers warn that rubber or latex-backed mats can discolour their floors. Whatever mat you use, lift it after the first week and look for marks before leaving it down long term.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

